.TH DIFFVIM-TMUX 1 "2026-08-16" "diffvim 1.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
diffvim-tmux \- animate a code diff in vim inside a tmux pane (bash + tmux variant)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diffvim-tmux
.RI [ options ]
.RI <oldfile>
.RI <newfile>
.br
.B diffvim-tmux
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-multi
.RI <old1:new1>
.RI <old2:new2>
\&...
.br
.B diffvim-tmux
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-replay
.RI <file>
.RB [ \-\-from
.IR REV ]
.RB [ \-\-to
.IR REV ]
.br
.B diffvim-tmux
.B \-\-version
.br
.B diffvim-tmux
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B diffvim-tmux
is the tmux-based variant of
.BR diffvim (1).
It runs vim inside a tmux pane and orchestrates the animation from a
separate bash process. The bash orchestrator computes the diff, drives the
animation state machine, and sends Ex commands to vim via
.BR "tmux send-keys" .
User input is delivered from vim to bash via a named pipe (FIFO).

This variant is useful when you want to script or extend the animation in
bash, or when you need the animation to run in a detachable tmux session.

Because the orchestrator and vim run in separate processes,
.B diffvim-tmux
can exhibit race conditions if bash queues keys faster than vim can
process them. For race-free animation, use
.B diffvim
instead.

.SH OPTIONS
.B diffvim-tmux
accepts the same options as
.BR diffvim (1),
with the same semantics. See
.BR diffvim (1)
for the full list. The only differences are:

.TP
.B \-\-scroll zz|zt|zb|none
Default is
.B none
in
.B diffvim-tmux
(because tmux already provides its own scrolling), versus
.B zz
in
.BR diffvim .

.TP
.B \-\-tmux-session NAME
Override the tmux session name (default: a unique name based on PID).

.SH CONTROLS
During the animation, the following normal-mode keys are available in vim:
.TP
.B Space
Pause / resume the animation.
.TP
.B n
Skip the current hunk (apply instantly, move to next).
.TP
.B b
Back to the previous hunk (revert and restart it).
.TP
.B q
Stop the animation (leave the buffer in its current state).
.TP
.B +
Speed up (multiply speed by 1.5).
.TP
.B \-
Slow down (divide speed by 1.5).
.TP
.B =
Reset speed to 1.0x.
.TP
.B ?
Show in-vim help.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
The same environment variables as
.BR diffvim (1)
apply. In addition:
.TP
.B DIFFVIM_TMUX_BIN
Path to the
.BR tmux (1)
binary (default:
.BR tmux ).

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/diffvim-fifo-$$
Named pipe used for vim \(->bash user input. Cleaned up on exit.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B diffvim-tmux old.py new.py
Animate the diff between
.I old.py
and
.I new.py
inside a fresh tmux session.
.TP
.B diffvim-tmux --speed 2 --word-diff a.py b.py
Animate at 2x speed with word-level diffing.
.TP
.B diffvim-tmux --replay main.py --from HEAD~10 --to HEAD
Animate the last 10 commits' changes to
.IR main.py .

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR diffvim (1),
.BR diffvim.pl (1),
.BR diffvim-compute (1),
.BR tmux (1)
.br
Full documentation in
.I docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
and the mdBook at
.IR docs/src/ .

> **Note:** The project now uses an external pipeline (diffvim-compute-cpp → diffvim-postprocess → diffvim-pace → animator). See `docs/PIPELINE.md` and `docs/DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md` for the current architecture. Coloring (`diffvim-colorize`), streaming mode (`--stream`), typed delays, and the ghost-line fix are described in the Developer Guide.
